Package: shadowscan
Title: Shadow-Variable Screens and Sharp Sensitivity Scans for Nonignorable Binary Outcomes
Version: 0.0.0.9000
Authors@R:
    person("Michael", "Bücker", email = "michael.buecker@fh-muenster.de",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: Two tools for a binary outcome that is missing not at random.
    'shadow_screen()' asks whether a candidate variable can serve as a shadow
    variable, reporting its relevance for the outcome and its violation of the
    exclusion restriction on a common scale (incremental McFadden R-squared).
    'shadow_scan()' asks how far the answer can move: given the observed-data
    outcome probability and the conditional observation rate, it returns the
    sharp identified set under a bound on the ratio of response propensities
    between outcome values, in closed form for a binary outcome
    ('shadow_scan()') and for the mean of any outcome with a finite mean
    ('shadow_scan_mean()'), pointwise and aggregated over a portfolio. The two tools
    answer different questions under different assumptions and are not two
    halves of one procedure.
URL: https://github.com/mchlbckr/shadowscan
BugReports: https://github.com/mchlbckr/shadowscan/issues
